{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NZSWTx6c8FWdzjhSEGcVHq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — gen-depth-fingerprint\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- **Primary: EMNLP 2026 Findings (ARR deadline: May 25, 2026)** — D012: 恢复为主投稿目标\n5\t- Positioning: **Findings paper**（非 main conference long paper）\n6\t- Fallback venues: ICLR 2027 (Sep 24), NAACL 2027 ARR (Oct 16)\n7\t\n8\t## Core Insight\n9\tNon-paraphrastic LLM generation chains exhibit **differential depth response** (DDR): **central-tendency/diversity features** (entropy_mean, TTR, hapax ratio) drift monotonically with depth, while **shape features** (entropy_max, tail_decay) remain near-stationary. This is consistent with recursive generation inducing a **location shift** in the NLL distribution while approximately preserving distributional shape. The contribution is: (a) the **task formulation** (generation depth classification beyond binary detection), (b) **empirical validation** across 8 generators / 5 model families (Pythia, OLMo, Qwen, Llama, Mistral; 0.6B–8B) showing DDR is robust, (c) a lightweight 7-feature ensemble achieving leak-free binary F1 = 0.694 under 8-gen LOGO. Depth signal degrades substantially under instruction tuning, restricting current scope to base models.\n10\t\n11\t## Core Claims (3 条，均可证伪)\n12\t\n13\t**Claim 1 — Depth Discriminability**: A 7-feature distributional ensemble can classify generation depth (gen-0 / gen-1 / gen-2+) with leak-free binary F1 = 0.694 (95% CI [0.688, 0.700]) under 8-generator LOGO evaluation, generalizing across unseen generators and model families (LOFO binary F1 = 0.686). Cross-model chains yield binary F1 = 0.830 under generator-level LOGO (5-gen subset). Primary metric is leak-free binary F1 (gen-1 vs gen-2+), excluding gen-0 to avoid seed-sharing leakage.\n14\t\n15\t**Claim 2 — Differential Depth Response & Feature Complementarity**: Features exhibit DDR: drifting features show |ρ| > 0.60 (hapax −0.63, TTR −0.61, entropy_mean −0.60), while shape features show |ρ| < 0.10 (tail_decay 0.04). KS statistics confirm: drifting 0.516–0.537 vs shape 0.043–0.052 (~10× gap). Full 7-feature set (10-fold CV F1 = 0.773) outperforms surface-only (0.688) and reference-only (0.713; McNemar p ≈ 0). Reference-only yields higher binary F1 (0.833 vs 0.695) but surface features are essential for 3-class gen-0 discrimination.\n16\t\n17\t**Claim 3 — Downstream Utility (Negative Finding)**: Depth-aware data curation does NOT improve downstream CLM quality. At equal sample count (n=3,951): random subsampling (ppl=2.367) outperforms depth 0–1 (2.387) and binary-filtered (2.389). TOST confirms equivalence at ±2% margin (p = 1.1×10⁻⁴). Full dataset (n=12,299, ppl=2.335) vs depth-filtered requires ±3% margin for equivalence. Depth fingerprinting is a **diagnostic** tool for provenance characterization, not a **curative** tool for data selection.\n18\t\n19\t## Headline Metrics Summary\n20\t\n21\t| Metric | Value | Context |\n22\t|--------|-------|---------|\n23\t| **Primary: LOGO binary F1** | **0.694** [0.688, 0.700] | 8-gen LOGO, gen-1 vs gen-2+, leak-free |\n24\t| LOGO 3-class F1 | 0.762 [0.758, 0.766] | Inflated by gen-0 seed sharing |\n25\t| Cross-model binary F1 | 0.830 | 5-gen subset, generator-level LOGO |\n26\t| Cross-model 3-class F1 | 0.789 | 5-gen subset, generator-level LOGO |\n27\t| LOFO binary F1 | 0.686 | Leave-one-family-out |\n28\t| Per-gen binary F1 range | 0.633–0.724 | Mistral-7B lowest, Pythia-2.8B highest |\n29\t| 9-gen LOGO 3-class F1 | 0.751 | +Gemma-2-2B; Gemma gen-1 F1=0.036 anomaly |\n30\t| DDR causality | 6/7 Granger sig, 5.5× perm imp gap | Drifting dominance confirmed |\n31\t| Feature ablation (10-fold CV) | Full 0.773, Surface 0.688, Ref 0.713 | McNemar p ≈ 0 |\n32\t| Length-matched binary F1 | 0.724 vs 0.770 unmatched | Deconfounding, not sample-size effect |\n33\t\n34\t## Scope & Limitations (必须在论文中突出)\n35\t\n36\t1. **Base models only**: Instruct-tuned models show substantially degraded signal (OLMo-Instruct LOGO F1 = 0.338 vs 0.763 base). Alignment-imposed length regulation and format constraints compress the distributional variation DDR exploits. This is the **primary scope limitation**.\n37\t2. **Temperature sensitivity**: Reliable within t ∈ [0.5, 0.9] (≤1 pp degradation from default t=0.7). At t = 1.0: −6 pp degradation; entropy_mean ρ drops from −0.60 to −0.002. Cross-temperature transfer degrades severely (t=0.7 → t=1.0: Qwen3-1.7B F1 drops 0.789 → 0.418).\n38\t3. **7-feature ensemble is not optimal**: Multi-detector ensembles (4-det + surface) achieve 3-class 0.795 vs 0.774 (+2.1 pp) and binary 0.708 vs 0.705 (+0.3 pp), but at 2.4× inference cost and 3.2× GPU memory. 7-feature positioned on **efficiency advantage**, not absolute performance.\n39\t4. **3-class resolution ceiling**: 5-class F1 drops to ~0.50; ordinal methods (CORAL, Frank-Hall) yield no improvement over standard XGBoost. Gen-1/gen-2+ boundary is the bottleneck (concentrates 86% of binary errors as FNs).\n40\t5. **Scale effect**: Larger generators show moderate degradation (Mistral-7B: 0.633, Qwen3-8B: 0.692 vs average 0.694). Higher-capacity models compress feature margins.\n41\t6. **Prompt-type dependence**: Continuation chains show stronger drift (F1 = 0.781) than elaboration (0.730).\n42\t\n43\t## Story Spine\n44\t\n45\t**Problem**: Web-scraped training data increasingly contains LLM-generated text of unknown provenance depth (7–17% of recent scientific papers). Current detection methods offer only binary real-vs-synthetic classification, conflating qualitatively different material: gen-1 retains much of the source distribution's lexical properties, while deeper text exhibits measurably reduced vocabulary diversity and compressed perplexity.\n46\t\n47\t**Existing approaches & gaps**: Binary detectors (FastDetectGPT, Binoculars, LD-Score) cannot distinguish gen-1 from gen-2+. Attractor cycle theory (ACL 2025) bounds paraphrasing chains to ~3 classes but depends on semantic-preservation constraints. No existing method addresses generation depth estimation in non-paraphrastic continuation chains. When repurposed as ordinal depth predictors, single detector scores show moderate signal but high cross-generator variance.\n48\t\n49\t**Our framing**: We formulate generation depth classification (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+) and characterize DDR across 8 base-model generators from 5 families (0.6B–8B). Central-tendency features drift monotonically while shape features remain near-stationary, consistent with recursive generation inducing a location shift in the NLL distribution. A 7-feature ensemble achieves leak-free binary F1 = 0.694 under 8-gen LOGO with a single 410M reference model. Multi-detector ensembles achieve marginally higher F1 at 2.4× cost. The signal degrades under instruction tuning and high temperature, restricting current scope to base models at moderate temperatures.\n50\t\n51\t**Headline result template**: \"On 8 base-model generators from 5 families (0.6B–8B), our 7-feature ensemble achieves leak-free binary F1 = 0.694 under LOGO evaluation and 0.830 on cross-model chains, using a single 410M reference model. Depth-aware curation is statistically equivalent to random subsampling for downstream CLM quality (TOST p = 1.1×10⁻⁴ at ±2% margin), establishing depth fingerprinting as a diagnostic tool for provenance characterization.\"\n52\t\n53\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n54\t\n55\t**RC1 — \"Is this just vocabulary impoverishment?\"**: Surface features alone achieve 0.688, but full features achieve 0.773 (McNemar p ≈ 0). Reference features capture different, complementary signal. Reference-only yields higher binary F1 (0.833) but misses gen-0 discrimination. The complementarity itself is a finding.\n56\t\n57\t**RC2 — \"Does this generalize beyond Pythia?\"**: 8 generators from 5 families (Pythia, OLMo, Qwen, Llama, Mistral). LOFO binary F1 = 0.686 confirms cross-family transfer. Cross-model chains (heterogeneous generators per depth step) yield binary F1 = 0.764.\n58\t\n59\t**RC3 — \"Is 3-class sufficient?\"**: 5-class F1 drops to ~0.50. Ordinal methods (CORAL, Frank-Hall) yield no improvement. Gen-1/gen-2+ boundary is the resolution bottleneck. 3 classes represent practical ceiling.\n60\t\n61\t**RC4 — \"How realistic are single-model chains?\"**: Cross-model chains (different generator per step) are evaluated and yield higher F1 (0.764 binary), suggesting generator heterogeneity amplifies distributional shifts.\n62\t\n63\t**RC5 — \"What about human editing?\"**: Robustness test with simulated human edits (random word replacement + LLM-simulated editing). Reported in appendix.\n64\t\n65\t**RC6 — \"Ecological validity?\"**: Multi-domain seeds (news, Wikipedia, scientific, creative) + multiple prompt types (continuation, elaboration, rewriting). Prompt-type dependence reported transparently (continuation 0.781 vs elaboration 0.730).\n66\t\n67\t**RC8 — \"Aren't these just OOD detection features?\"**: Contribution is NOT the features but: (a) ordinal depth estimation formulation, (b) empirical DDR discovery (drifting vs stationary), (c) Findings-level empirical characterization. Paper explicitly connects to OOD/perplexity-based detection literature.\n68\t\n69\t**RC9 — \"Only base models — what about instruct?\"**: Acknowledged as primary limitation. OLMo-Instruct LOGO F1 = 0.338 vs 0.763 base. Instruction tuning compresses the distributional variation DDR exploits. Reported transparently; defines scope boundary.\n70\t\n71\t**RC10 — \"Text length confound\"**: Length-matched subsampling: binary F1 = 0.724 (vs 0.770 unmatched). Random-subsample control confirms drop reflects deconfounding, not sample-size reduction.\n72\t\n73\t**RC11 — \"Downstream — limited evidence\"**: Three seeds, single perplexity metric. TOST equivalence confirmed at ±2% margin for equal-count comparison. Margin sensitivity analysis across ±1%–5% reported. Cohen's d inflated by low seed-level variance; TOST is the appropriate test.\n74\t\n75\t## Contribution Positioning (Findings Level)\n76\tThe contribution is **empirical and conceptual**, appropriate for Findings:\n77\t1. **Problem formulation**: Generation depth classification as a new task beyond binary detection (no prior work addresses this in non-paraphrastic chains)\n78\t2. **Empirical characterization**: DDR across 8 generators / 5 families (0.6B–8B), with cross-generator generalization (LOGO binary F1 = 0.694, LOFO 0.686, cross-model 0.764). Honest reporting of scope boundaries (instruct degradation, temperature sensitivity, scale effect).\n79\t3. **Negative finding**: Depth-aware curation ≡ random subsampling for downstream quality (TOST confirmed). Depth fingerprinting is diagnostic, not curative.\n80\t\n81\t**Framing discipline:**\n82\t- Do NOT call the drift/stationarity split a \"discovery\" — it follows from location-shift properties of the NLL distribution. Acknowledge explicitly.\n83\t- Do NOT use H1/H2 terminology. Use \"drifting\" vs \"stationary\" or \"differential response.\"\n84\t- Do NOT overclaim \"dual dynamics.\" Use \"differential depth response\" (DDR).\n85\t- Report **realistic performance range** (binary F1 0.633–0.724 per-generator, 0.694 LOGO average) in abstract/conclusion, not best-case.\n86\t- Primary metric is leak-free binary F1 (not 3-class, which is inflated by gen-0 seed sharing).\n87\t- 7-feature ensemble is positioned on efficiency, not absolute superiority over multi-detector ensembles.\n88\t\n89\tRelated work must cover: perplexity-based detection (DetectGPT, GLTR, DNA-GPT, Binoculars), model collapse theory, OOD detection, data quality scoring, watermarking, vocabulary diversity, stylometry/authorship attribution, iterative generation (LLM as Broken Telephone ACL 2025), membership inference.\n90\t\n91\t## Venue Fit\n92\t- EMNLP 2026 Findings: Excellent fit — directly addresses data contamination, synthetic data feedback loops, data governance. Empirical/conceptual contribution with honest scope boundaries. Findings-appropriate ambition level: novel task formulation + solid characterization, not a state-of-the-art methodology claim.\n93\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a6e7dae-2371-44f2-a86f-776f12713121","uuid":"35340d44-9df9-40fa-a5fa-576845313c8c","timestamp":"2026-05-20T23:58:37.587Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_3/projects/gen-depth-fingerprint/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T232427/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — gen-depth-fingerprint\n\n## Target Venue\n- **Primary: EMNLP 2026 Findings (ARR deadline: May 25, 2026)** — D012: 恢复为主投稿目标\n- Positioning: **Findings paper**（非 main conference long paper）\n- Fallback venues: ICLR 2027 (Sep 24), NAACL 2027 ARR (Oct 16)\n\n## Core Insight\nNon-paraphrastic LLM generation chains exhibit **differential depth response** (DDR): **central-tendency/diversity features** (entropy_mean, TTR, hapax ratio) drift monotonically with depth, while **shape features** (entropy_max, tail_decay) remain near-stationary. This is consistent with recursive generation inducing a **location shift** in the NLL distribution while approximately preserving distributional shape. The contribution is: (a) the **task formulation** (generation depth classification beyond binary detection), (b) **empirical validation** across 8 generators / 5 model families (Pythia, OLMo, Qwen, Llama, Mistral; 0.6B–8B) showing DDR is robust, (c) a lightweight 7-feature ensemble achieving leak-free binary F1 = 0.694 under 8-gen LOGO. Depth signal degrades substantially under instruction tuning, restricting current scope to base models.\n\n## Core Claims (3 条，均可证伪)\n\n**Claim 1 — Depth Discriminability**: A 7-feature distributional ensemble can classify generation depth (gen-0 / gen-1 / gen-2+) with leak-free binary F1 = 0.694 (95% CI [0.688, 0.700]) under 8-generator LOGO evaluation, generalizing across unseen generators and model families (LOFO binary F1 = 0.686). Cross-model chains yield binary F1 = 0.830 under generator-level LOGO (5-gen subset). Primary metric is leak-free binary F1 (gen-1 vs gen-2+), excluding gen-0 to avoid seed-sharing leakage.\n\n**Claim 2 — Differential Depth Response & Feature Complementarity**: Features exhibit DDR: drifting features show |ρ| > 0.60 (hapax −0.63, TTR −0.61, entropy_mean −0.60), while shape features show |ρ| < 0.10 (tail_decay 0.04). KS statistics confirm: drifting 0.516–0.537 vs shape 0.043–0.052 (~10× gap). Full 7-feature set (10-fold CV F1 = 0.773) outperforms surface-only (0.688) and reference-only (0.713; McNemar p ≈ 0). Reference-only yields higher binary F1 (0.833 vs 0.695) but surface features are essential for 3-class gen-0 discrimination.\n\n**Claim 3 — Downstream Utility (Negative Finding)**: Depth-aware data curation does NOT improve downstream CLM quality. At equal sample count (n=3,951): random subsampling (ppl=2.367) outperforms depth 0–1 (2.387) and binary-filtered (2.389). TOST confirms equivalence at ±2% margin (p = 1.1×10⁻⁴). Full dataset (n=12,299, ppl=2.335) vs depth-filtered requires ±3% margin for equivalence. Depth fingerprinting is a **diagnostic** tool for provenance characterization, not a **curative** tool for data selection.\n\n## Headline Metrics Summary\n\n| Metric | Value | Context |\n|--------|-------|---------|\n| **Primary: LOGO binary F1** | **0.694** [0.688, 0.700] | 8-gen LOGO, gen-1 vs gen-2+, leak-free |\n| LOGO 3-class F1 | 0.762 [0.758, 0.766] | Inflated by gen-0 seed sharing |\n| Cross-model binary F1 | 0.830 | 5-gen subset, generator-level LOGO |\n| Cross-model 3-class F1 | 0.789 | 5-gen subset, generator-level LOGO |\n| LOFO binary F1 | 0.686 | Leave-one-family-out |\n| Per-gen binary F1 range | 0.633–0.724 | Mistral-7B lowest, Pythia-2.8B highest |\n| 9-gen LOGO 3-class F1 | 0.751 | +Gemma-2-2B; Gemma gen-1 F1=0.036 anomaly |\n| DDR causality | 6/7 Granger sig, 5.5× perm imp gap | Drifting dominance confirmed |\n| Feature ablation (10-fold CV) | Full 0.773, Surface 0.688, Ref 0.713 | McNemar p ≈ 0 |\n| Length-matched binary F1 | 0.724 vs 0.770 unmatched | Deconfounding, not sample-size effect |\n\n## Scope & Limitations (必须在论文中突出)\n\n1. **Base models only**: Instruct-tuned models show substantially degraded signal (OLMo-Instruct LOGO F1 = 0.338 vs 0.763 base). Alignment-imposed length regulation and format constraints compress the distributional variation DDR exploits. This is the **primary scope limitation**.\n2. **Temperature sensitivity**: Reliable within t ∈ [0.5, 0.9] (≤1 pp degradation from default t=0.7). At t = 1.0: −6 pp degradation; entropy_mean ρ drops from −0.60 to −0.002. Cross-temperature transfer degrades severely (t=0.7 → t=1.0: Qwen3-1.7B F1 drops 0.789 → 0.418).\n3. **7-feature ensemble is not optimal**: Multi-detector ensembles (4-det + surface) achieve 3-class 0.795 vs 0.774 (+2.1 pp) and binary 0.708 vs 0.705 (+0.3 pp), but at 2.4× inference cost and 3.2× GPU memory. 7-feature positioned on **efficiency advantage**, not absolute performance.\n4. **3-class resolution ceiling**: 5-class F1 drops to ~0.50; ordinal methods (CORAL, Frank-Hall) yield no improvement over standard XGBoost. Gen-1/gen-2+ boundary is the bottleneck (concentrates 86% of binary errors as FNs).\n5. **Scale effect**: Larger generators show moderate degradation (Mistral-7B: 0.633, Qwen3-8B: 0.692 vs average 0.694). Higher-capacity models compress feature margins.\n6. **Prompt-type dependence**: Continuation chains show stronger drift (F1 = 0.781) than elaboration (0.730).\n\n## Story Spine\n\n**Problem**: Web-scraped training data increasingly contains LLM-generated text of unknown provenance depth (7–17% of recent scientific papers). Current detection methods offer only binary real-vs-synthetic classification, conflating qualitatively different material: gen-1 retains much of the source distribution's lexical properties, while deeper text exhibits measurably reduced vocabulary diversity and compressed perplexity.\n\n**Existing approaches & gaps**: Binary detectors (FastDetectGPT, Binoculars, LD-Score) cannot distinguish gen-1 from gen-2+. Attractor cycle theory (ACL 2025) bounds paraphrasing chains to ~3 classes but depends on semantic-preservation constraints. No existing method addresses generation depth estimation in non-paraphrastic continuation chains. When repurposed as ordinal depth predictors, single detector scores show moderate signal but high cross-generator variance.\n\n**Our framing**: We formulate generation depth classification (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+) and characterize DDR across 8 base-model generators from 5 families (0.6B–8B). Central-tendency features drift monotonically while shape features remain near-stationary, consistent with recursive generation inducing a location shift in the NLL distribution. A 7-feature ensemble achieves leak-free binary F1 = 0.694 under 8-gen LOGO with a single 410M reference model. Multi-detector ensembles achieve marginally higher F1 at 2.4× cost. The signal degrades under instruction tuning and high temperature, restricting current scope to base models at moderate temperatures.\n\n**Headline result template**: \"On 8 base-model generators from 5 families (0.6B–8B), our 7-feature ensemble achieves leak-free binary F1 = 0.694 under LOGO evaluation and 0.830 on cross-model chains, using a single 410M reference model. Depth-aware curation is statistically equivalent to random subsampling for downstream CLM quality (TOST p = 1.1×10⁻⁴ at ±2% margin), establishing depth fingerprinting as a diagnostic tool for provenance characterization.\"\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n**RC1 — \"Is this just vocabulary impoverishment?\"**: Surface features alone achieve 0.688, but full features achieve 0.773 (McNemar p ≈ 0). Reference features capture different, complementary signal. Reference-only yields higher binary F1 (0.833) but misses gen-0 discrimination. The complementarity itself is a finding.\n\n**RC2 — \"Does this generalize beyond Pythia?\"**: 8 generators from 5 families (Pythia, OLMo, Qwen, Llama, Mistral). LOFO binary F1 = 0.686 confirms cross-family transfer. Cross-model chains (heterogeneous generators per depth step) yield binary F1 = 0.764.\n\n**RC3 — \"Is 3-class sufficient?\"**: 5-class F1 drops to ~0.50. Ordinal methods (CORAL, Frank-Hall) yield no improvement. Gen-1/gen-2+ boundary is the resolution bottleneck. 3 classes represent practical ceiling.\n\n**RC4 — \"How realistic are single-model chains?\"**: Cross-model chains (different generator per step) are evaluated and yield higher F1 (0.764 binary), suggesting generator heterogeneity amplifies distributional shifts.\n\n**RC5 — \"What about human editing?\"**: Robustness test with simulated human edits (random word replacement + LLM-simulated editing). Reported in appendix.\n\n**RC6 — \"Ecological validity?\"**: Multi-domain seeds (news, Wikipedia, scientific, creative) + multiple prompt types (continuation, elaboration, rewriting). Prompt-type dependence reported transparently (continuation 0.781 vs elaboration 0.730).\n\n**RC8 — \"Aren't these just OOD detection features?\"**: Contribution is NOT the features but: (a) ordinal depth estimation formulation, (b) empirical DDR discovery (drifting vs stationary), (c) Findings-level empirical characterization. Paper explicitly connects to OOD/perplexity-based detection literature.\n\n**RC9 — \"Only base models — what about instruct?\"**: Acknowledged as primary limitation. OLMo-Instruct LOGO F1 = 0.338 vs 0.763 base. Instruction tuning compresses the distributional variation DDR exploits. Reported transparently; defines scope boundary.\n\n**RC10 — \"Text length confound\"**: Length-matched subsampling: binary F1 = 0.724 (vs 0.770 unmatched). Random-subsample control confirms drop reflects deconfounding, not sample-size reduction.\n\n**RC11 — \"Downstream — limited evidence\"**: Three seeds, single perplexity metric. TOST equivalence confirmed at ±2% margin for equal-count comparison. Margin sensitivity analysis across ±1%–5% reported. Cohen's d inflated by low seed-level variance; TOST is the appropriate test.\n\n## Contribution Positioning (Findings Level)\nThe contribution is **empirical and conceptual**, appropriate for Findings:\n1. **Problem formulation**: Generation depth classification as a new task beyond binary detection (no prior work addresses this in non-paraphrastic chains)\n2. **Empirical characterization**: DDR across 8 generators / 5 families (0.6B–8B), with cross-generator generalization (LOGO binary F1 = 0.694, LOFO 0.686, cross-model 0.764). Honest reporting of scope boundaries (instruct degradation, temperature sensitivity, scale effect).\n3. **Negative finding**: Depth-aware curation ≡ random subsampling for downstream quality (TOST confirmed). Depth fingerprinting is diagnostic, not curative.\n\n**Framing discipline:**\n- Do NOT call the drift/stationarity split a \"discovery\" — it follows from location-shift properties of the NLL distribution. Acknowledge explicitly.\n- Do NOT use H1/H2 terminology. Use \"drifting\" vs \"stationary\" or \"differential response.\"\n- Do NOT overclaim \"dual dynamics.\" Use \"differential depth response\" (DDR).\n- Report **realistic performance range** (binary F1 0.633–0.724 per-generator, 0.694 LOGO average) in abstract/conclusion, not best-case.\n- Primary metric is leak-free binary F1 (not 3-class, which is inflated by gen-0 seed sharing).\n- 7-feature ensemble is positioned on efficiency, not absolute superiority over multi-detector ensembles.\n\nRelated work must cover: perplexity-based detection (DetectGPT, GLTR, DNA-GPT, Binoculars), model collapse theory, OOD detection, data quality scoring, watermarking, vocabulary diversity, stylometry/authorship attribution, iterative generation (LLM as Broken Telephone ACL 2025), membership inference.\n\n## Venue Fit\n- EMNLP 2026 Findings: Excellent fit — directly addresses data contamination, synthetic data feedback loops, data governance. Empirical/conceptual contribution with honest scope boundaries. Findings-appropriate ambition level: novel task formulation + solid characterization, not a state-of-the-art methodology claim.\n","numLines":93,"startLine":1,"totalLines":93}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CX332JLDdScoBZQQ4qChx1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have both the experiment data and the paper framing. Let me also check if the output directory exists.\n\nLet me now write the full LaTeX paper. I need to cover all 12 experiments from the JSON data:\n\n1. feature_ablation_and_trend - Feature ablation + trend analysis\n2. cross_generator_evaluation - 5-gen LOGO evaluation\n3. cross_family_reference_model - Cross-reference-model DDR test\n4. downstream_utility - Negative downstream finding\n5. prompt_type_comparison - Continuation vs elaboration vs rewriting\n6. paraphrastic_comparison - Paraphrastic vs non-paraphrastic\n\n\n7. cross_model_chains - Cross-model chains\n8. multi_domain_seeds - Multi-domain evaluation\n9. finer_grained_ordinal - 5-class ordinal classification\n10. human_edit_robustness - Human edit robustness\n11. ld_score_feature - LD-Score feature analysis\n12. end_to_end_classifier - QLoRA end-to-end classifier\n\nI see there are actually 15 experiments listed in the data, including text_length_control, large_scale_generator, and ordinal_baseline_comparison. Let me organize these into the paper structure.\n\nI'm planning the main body to cover the core contributions: the DDR framework and its evaluation across multiple generators, the analysis of drifting versus stationary features, baseline comparisons with existing detectors, and the downstream utility findings. The appendix will hold the more specialized experiments like feature ablations, prompt-type variations, and the robustness tests.\n\nNow I'm setting up the LaTeX template and compiling the key results—the LOGO and LOFO F1 scores across different evaluation settings, the cross-model performance at 0.830, and the feature ablation breakdown showing how much the reference-based features contribute compared to surface-level ones. I'm noticing some discrepancies between the paper framing and the experiment data—the JSON shows 5-generator results while the framing references 8-generator numbers, so I'll rely on the JSON for specific experiments and use the framing's headline figures where they align. Now I need to structure the paper carefully, making sure I only use numbers that actually exist in the data, cover all 15 experiments, and organize the results so the most important findings are in the main body with supporting details in appendices. I'll keep the writing compilable with pdflatex and follow the framing's guidance on emphasis. Finishing out the appendix sections with the remaining experiments, then I'm ready to write the full paper using a two-column ACL-style format.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19925,"cache_read_input_tokens":25198,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19925},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a6e7dae-2371-44f2-a86f-776f12713121","uuid":"395c135b-ff75-447c-a73e-72734403acad"}
